#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068488 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b89c50f3-2181-5b61-96cb-386ef88d2035" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by Its Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "e2579380-1933-5819-a39e-9ffcfdafeeee" :question/math-academy-id "e-452" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int 6x^2(2x^3+3)^6\\,\\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "We note that $6x^2$ is the derivative of the quantity in the parentheses. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu=2x^3+3\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{du}{dx} &= 6x^{2}⟹du = 6x^{2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int6x^{2}(2x^{3} + 3)^{6}dx &= \\int(2x^{3} + 3)^{6}6x^{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\intu^{6}du\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\intu^{6}du &= \\frac{u^{7}}{7} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute\n\n$$\nu=2x^3+3\n$$\n\nback in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int 6x^2(2x^3+3)^6\\,\\textrm{d}x =\\dfrac{(2x^3+3)^7}{7}+C\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068489 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fe2a279-0b92-561e-b17d-b902f43725ac" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by a Constant Multiple of Its Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "9bf7d343-8d3d-5194-93c4-7b80bc1e3609" :question/math-academy-id "e-453" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int x\\left(1+2x^2\\right)^6\\,\\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "The $x$ that's outside the parentheses isn't quite the derivative of $(1+2x^2)$. The derivative of $(1+2x^2)$ is $4x$, so we're out by a constant factor of $4$. However, since we're out only by a constant factor, substitution will still work!\n\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu=1+2x^2\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{du}{dx} &= 4x⟹\\frac{1}{4}du = xdx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\intx(1 + 2x^{2})^{6}dx &= \\int(1 + 2x^{2})^{6} \\cdot xdx \\\\\n&= \\intu^{6} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\\intu^{6}du\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{4}\\intu^{6}du &= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{u^{7}}{7} + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{28}u^{7} + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{28}(1 + 2x^{2})^{7} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068490 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18ce1807-3a09-5ebd-b3cd-a91db8d2b4a8" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Using Natural Logarithms"}] :question/id #uuid "49f679c4-bbc4-59a5-9b0c-0f3b69d7cf57" :question/math-academy-id "e-454" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{x^2}{x^3+1}\\,\\textrm d x$." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nu=x^3+1\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3x^2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u= x^2 \\, \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{3} + 1}dx &= \\int\\frac{1}{x^{3} + 1} \\cdot x^{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\int\\frac{1}{u} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\int\\frac{1}{u}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}u & + C \\\\ = \\frac{1}{3}\\ln & x^{3} + 1\\end{vmatrix}+ C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068552 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "03e934c8-da65-5615-9717-a50eee3577fd" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Other Fractional Expressions"}] :question/id #uuid "0461f8ba-c3c5-5e22-a679-32ee4f2a01b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-6149" :question/problem "$\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{x^2}{\\sqrt{1-x^3}} \\, \\textrm{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nu = 1 - x^3\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -3x^2 \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac{1}{3} \\textrm d u= x^2 \\, \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{x^{2}}{\\sqrt{1 - x^{3}}}dx &= \\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - x^{3}}} \\cdot x^{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}} \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})du \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{3}\\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}du \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{3}(2\\sqrt{u}) + C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{u} + C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{1 - x^{3}} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fe2a279-0b92-561e-b17d-b902f43725ac" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by a Constant Multiple of Its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bba25e9-ecce-5cf6-ba21-842081683042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40}(x^{4} + 11)^{10} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065525} {:answer/id #uuid "eca1a03a-fc27-5da6-a150-e15fd02eb36f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{36}(x^{4} + 11)^{9} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065526} {:answer/id #uuid "0b0a7d0f-dcbf-5947-a080-fd1cf6aea5db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{44}(x^{4} + 11)^{10} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065527} {:answer/id #uuid "229c4b0b-a6bc-5362-a22b-74fa71ae5862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}(x^{4} + 11)^{10} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065528} {:answer/id #uuid "492128b8-b7ff-540c-846d-adbf8b42333e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}(x^{4} + 11)^{9} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bba25e9-ecce-5cf6-ba21-842081683042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40}(x^{4} + 11)^{10} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218606552